Scenario ID: S05
Scenario title: Specification Document Review
Initial workflow description:
An engineer reviews a specification document to check whether new features are reflected correctly and whether there are typos or missing details.

Domain expert answers:
{
  "answers": [
    {
      "question_id": "Q1",
      "question": "What event or condition triggers the engineer to begin reviewing a specification document — for example, a notification from a document management system, a new version being published, or a manual request?",
      "answer": "The trigger is not explicitly specified in the workflow. The process begins when the document reviewer collects the specification document and the related feature change list, implying a handoff or assignment has occurred, but the exact triggering mechanism — such as a notification or manual request — is not defined."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q2",
      "question": "In which system or location does the specification document live, and how does the engineer access the version that needs to be reviewed?",
      "answer": "The specification document resides in a document repository. The reviewer retrieves it from there along with the feature change list and the previous document version. The specific access method or version control mechanism is not further detailed."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q3",
      "question": "Does the engineer follow a defined checklist or set of criteria when reviewing the document, and if so, what are the main items on that list?",
      "answer": "Yes, a review checklist is used as an input. The main review criteria are: (1) verifying that all feature changes are reflected in the document, and (2) checking terminology and formatting consistency. Specific checklist line items beyond these two areas are not specified."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q4",
      "question": "What are the possible outcomes at the end of the review, and what action does the engineer take for each outcome — for example, approving the document, requesting changes, or escalating to someone else?",
      "answer": "Two decision points exist. First, if feature changes are missing, revisions are requested; if complete, the process continues. Second, after the document author revises the document, the approver either approves the final document or requests another revision. The feature owner also confirms revisions before final approval."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q5",
      "question": "How does the engineer record and communicate review findings — for instance, through inline comments in the document, a separate feedback form, a tracking ticket, or an email?",
      "answer": "The reviewer records comments and requested revisions using a commenting tool, producing review comments and a change request list. A change request tracker is also part of the system landscape. Specific communication methods such as email or forms are not mentioned."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q6",
      "question": "Is the review performed solely by one engineer, or do other roles participate, and is there a defined process if reviewers disagree or if no response is received within a certain timeframe?",
      "answer": "Multiple roles participate: the document reviewer, document author, feature owner, and approver. The reviewer raises comments, the author revises, the feature owner confirms, and the approver gives final sign-off. Handling of disagreements or non-response timeframes is not specified; unresolved comments are listed as an exception."
    }
  ]
}
